Not only those.
Usenet contains far more information than I'm willing to read. That's 
why I only subscribe to groups that are likely to contain stuff I 
want to read.

But even those newsgroups often contain quite a lot of stuff I'm not 
interested in. Here in r.g.r.n tht includes all postings about 
Slash'Em, because I only play vanilla nethack. My newsreader 
automatically filters out all messages with "[S]" or "Slash'em" in 
the subject. And no, I don't think that's a loss.

Furthermore, there are certain behaviours that suggest that authors 
of some articles don't want me to read their opinions. Everybody 
should keep in mind that I'm in no way obliged to read their articles 
(and no one else is, either) and that I (and many others) will simply 
skip an article if it's too hard to follow. This applies to articles 
with poor spelling, overuse of exclamation marks, missing 
punctuation, and, yes, top posting. My news software can't filter 
those automatically, but when I encounter people that produce 
particularly poor quality articles, I can downscore them manually.
 
This results in their (future) posts appearing below the other 
postings, so I hardly ever come around to reading them. This scoring 
is most often temporar, so after a month or so their articles are 
treated as normal and so their authors get another chance.

In German newsgroups, it's customary to use your full name. So, any 
article in German usenet that doesn't contain at least two words in 
the From: field is scored down by my newsreader similarily.

The really interesting part is that I found out that articles with 
poor appearance almost always also have poor contents.

